<nav aria-label="On this page" class="scripture-jumps"><a href="#foundations">Shruti &amp; Smriti</a><a href="#vedas">The four Vedas</a><a href="#library">Explore the texts</a><a href="#begin">Where to begin</a></nav>
<section class="section scripture-foundations" id="foundations"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 / FINDING YOUR BEARINGS</p><h2>Many texts.<br/><em>Many ways of learning.</em></h2><p>These categories offer a starting map. The authority and interpretation of a text also depend on the tradition reading it.</p></div><div class="scripture-pair"><article><span class="scripture-term" lang="sa">श्रुति</span><h3>Shruti</h3><p class="term-gloss">“That which is heard”</p><p>The Vedic corpus is revered as revealed knowledge. It includes the Upanishads.  </p></article><article><span class="scripture-term" lang="sa">स्मृति</span><h3>Smriti</h3><p class="term-gloss">“That which is remembered”</p><p>A broad category including epics and Puranas, conveying teachings through narrative and reflection. </p></article></div></section>
<section class="section veda-section" id="vedas"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / THE VEDIC FOUNDATION</p><h2>Four Vedas.<br/><em>A heritage of sound.</em></h2></div><p>Preserved through oral recitation, Vedic texts are part of a living chanting tradition. </p></div><div class="veda-grid"><article><span>01 / HYMNS</span><h3>Rig Veda</h3><p>A collection of sacred hymns.</p></article><article><span>02 / MELODY</span><h3>Sama Veda</h3><p>Hymns arranged for chanting, drawing extensively on the Rig Veda.</p></article><article><span>03 / RITUAL</span><h3>Yajur Veda</h3><p>Prayers and formulae connected with sacrificial rites.</p></article><article><span>04 / INVOCATIONS</span><h3>Atharva Veda</h3><p>Hymns, incantations and spells across a varied collection.</p></article></div><details class="veda-layers"><summary>What is inside the Vedic corpus?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div><p>A common introduction distinguishes four kinds of literature: <strong>Samhitas</strong> (hymns and mantras), <strong>Brahmanas</strong> (ritual explanations), <strong>Aranyakas</strong> (forest treatises) and <strong>Upanishads</strong> (spiritual and philosophical teachings). The Vedas contain more than hymns alone. </p></div></details></section>
<section class="section scripture-library" id="library"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / EXPLORE THE LIBRARY</p><h2>Follow your <em>curiosity.</em></h2></div><p>Start with a short introduction. Open a card for context and a place to read further.</p></div><div class="scripture-library-grid">
<article class="scripture-card" id="upanishads"><figure><img alt="Illustrative scene of a teacher and adult students learning beneath a banyan tree beside a river." decoding="async" height="800" loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/scripture-forest.webp" width="1200"/><figcaption>AI-GENERATED ILLUSTRATION</figcaption></figure><div class="scripture-card-copy"><p class="eyebrow">SELF / REALITY / LIBERATION</p><h3>The Upanishads</h3><p>Questions about the self, ultimate reality and liberation within the Vedic tradition. </p><details><summary>Look closer<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div><p>The Upanishads are part of the Vedic corpus. Their teachings underpin different Vedanta interpretations; a commentary helps reveal which viewpoint you are reading.</p><a href="https://www.swami-krishnananda.org/brdup/brhad_III-09.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Read a Brihadaranyaka dialogue ↗</a></div></details></div></article>
<article class="scripture-card" id="gita"><figure><img alt="Krishna teaching Arjuna on their chariot before the battle" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/gita-teaching.webp"/><figcaption>BHAGAVAD GITA · AI-GENERATED ILLUSTRATION</figcaption></figure><div class="scripture-card-copy"><p class="eyebrow">ACTION / KNOWLEDGE / DEVOTION</p><h3>The Bhagavad Gita</h3><p>Krishna’s dialogue with Arjuna on spiritual life amid a difficult decision. </p><details><summary>Look closer<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div><p>The Gita belongs to the Mahabharata. It is often read on its own, with commentaries that offer different understandings of its teachings. </p><a href="https://www.gitasupersite.iitk.ac.in/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Explore IIT Kanpur’s Gita Supersite ↗</a></div></details></div></article>
<article class="scripture-card" id="epics"><figure><img alt="A reader studying palm-leaf manuscripts beside a bow, quiver and lamp in a forest hermitage" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/ramayana-manuscript.webp"/><figcaption>RAMAYANA · AI-GENERATED ILLUSTRATION</figcaption></figure><div class="scripture-card-copy"><p class="eyebrow">ITIHASA / THE GREAT EPICS</p><h3>Ramayana &amp; Mahabharata</h3><p>Epic journeys through devotion, duty, family and conflict. </p><details><summary>Look closer<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div><p>The Ramayana follows Rama’s life and trials. The Mahabharata centres on a conflict between related families and explores difficult questions of dharma. Retellings bring distinct voices: Valmiki’s Ramayana and Tulsidas’s Ramcharitmanas are different works.</p><a href="https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Explore the Valmiki Ramayana ↗</a><a href="https://www.ramcharitmanas.iitk.ac.in/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Explore the Ramcharitmanas ↗</a></div></details></div></article>
<article class="scripture-card" id="puranas"><figure><img alt="Vishnu holding conch, discus, mace and lotus, seated on Shesha above the cosmic ocean" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/vishnu-purana.webp"/><figcaption>VISHNU · AI-GENERATED ILLUSTRATION</figcaption></figure><div class="scripture-card-copy"><p class="eyebrow">DEITIES / COSMOS / SACRED STORIES</p><h3>The Puranas</h3><p>Collections of sacred narratives that express teachings through stories. </p><details><summary>Look closer<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div><p>Tradition commonly names eighteen major Puranas, alongside many other Puranic works. This is a classification, not a count of all Hindu scriptures. Each story identifies the text and version it draws from.</p><a href="/lalita-bhandasura.html">Read Lalita and Bhandasura</a><a href="https://www.hinduamerican.org/hinduism-basics" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Read the scripture overview ↗</a></div></details></div></article></div></section>
<section class="scripture-agamas" id="agamas"><figure><img alt="Photograph of a white stone temple with ornate carvings and a golden shikhara" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/temple-photograph.webp"/><figcaption>CONTRIBUTED TEMPLE PHOTOGRAPH</figcaption></figure><div><p class="eyebrow">04 / TEXTS &amp; LIVING PRACTICE</p><h2>Agamas<br/><em>&amp; Tantras.</em></h2><p>These bodies of literature connect philosophy with worship, meditation, mantra, temple building and sacred imagery. Their teachings and authority are understood within particular lineages. </p><p>Shaiva, Vaishnava and Shakta traditions have their own textual heritages. These names cover diverse works rather than a single book or one uniform teaching.</p><a href="https://www.dlshq.org/teachings/tantra-yoga/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Explore a traditional introduction ↗</a></div></section>
<section class="section scripture-begin" id="begin"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">05 / YOUR FIRST STEP</p><h2>What draws <em>you in?</em></h2></div><p>You do not need to read everything in order. Choose one doorway and give it time.</p></div><div class="reading-paths"><a href="#gita"><span>I HAVE QUESTIONS ABOUT LIFE</span><h3>Begin with the Gita</h3><p>Read a short passage with a named translation and commentary.</p><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a href="#epics"><span>I WANT A STORY</span><h3>Enter the epics</h3><p>Meet the characters and follow one narrative at a time.</p><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a href="#upanishads"><span>I WANT TO THINK MORE DEEPLY</span><h3>Explore the Upanishads</h3><p>Take one dialogue slowly, with its philosophical context.</p><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a></div></section>
